.TH "SKARN-CHECK" "1" "2026-06-23" "skarn 0.33.0" "Skarn Manual"
.SH NAME
skarn\-check \- Scan AI sessions for leaked credentials and attack patterns
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBskarn\fR \fBcheck\fR [options]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Scan AI sessions for leaked credentials and attack patterns.
.PP
Control the security scan: which rules run, the scan window, the output
format, and CI gating. check needs a license and exits 7 before scanning
without a usable one. The free license is issued at https://getskarn.com/free
after an email confirmation, and skarn verifies it offline. Only
\-\-audit\-verify is exempt. The policy file (\-\-policy), the org baseline
directory that \-\-baseline unions and \-\-baseline\-merge consolidates, and
the hash\-chained audit log (\-\-audit\-log) are Pro capabilities of check:
check reads the policy file and the org baseline directory, and it reads the
audit log's chain head before appending this scan's record. When it decides a
verdict, skarn guard consults only the personal accepted\-findings file.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-rules\fR \fIpath\fR
Load a custom TOML rules file in addition to the bundled rules.
.TP
\fB\-\-ai\-rules\fR \fIpath\fR
Load an AI\-specific rules file.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-ai\-rules\fR
Disable the AI\-specific rules.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-default\-rules\fR
Disable the bundled detection rules.
.TP
\fB\-\-feed\fR \fIpath\fR
Apply a signed maintained\-feed bundle over the baseline rules. Requires a Pro
license.
.TP
\fB\-\-update\-rules\fR
Fetch and verify the latest feed from the subscriber channel, then scan.
Requires a Pro license.
.TP
\fB\-\-feed\-url\fR \fIurl\fR
Feed channel URL for \-\-update\-rules; falls back to $SKARN_FEED_URL. It must
be https, with plain http accepted only against loopback, and must carry no
userinfo. Requires a Pro license.
.TP
\fB\-\-offline\fR
Disable all network access (the maintained\-feed fetch).
.TP
\fB\-\-severity\fR \fIlevel\fR
Report only findings at or above this severity. One of: low, medium, high,
critical. The default is medium.
.TP
\fB\-\-canary\-file\fR \fIpath\fR
File of planted canary tokens, one per line; a match is a proven breach.
.TP
\fB\-\-check\-packages\fR
Flag typosquatted or URL\-sourced package installs found in sessions
(offline).
.TP
\fB\-\-check\-code\fR
Flag insecure code written by the assistant during the session.
.TP
\fB\-\-hours\fR \fIn\fR
Scan window in hours; 0 means no time limit. The default is 720.
.TP
\fB\-\-cli\fR \fIname\fR
Restrict the scan to one assistant's sessions. If this machine has no tool by
that name, skarn exits 6 rather than scanning nothing. A custom tool declared
in ~/.skarn.json is accepted by its configured name. One of: claude, gemini,
codex, cursor, copilot, kimi, grok, grokbot, antigravity, opencode, cline.
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIpath\fR
Restrict the scan to matching projects: a comma\-separated list of names or
paths, matched case\-insensitively against a session's project display name,
its canonical root path, or a parent directory of that root. A session read
from a Compliance API export carries no project, so any \-\-project value
excludes it.
.TP
\fB\-\-session\fR \fIid\fR
Scan a single session by id.
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fIfmt\fR
Output format. ndjson is an ECS\-shaped event stream, one redacted finding per
line, for SIEM ingest. A finding read from a Compliance API export carries the
user id as user.id and the workspace as skarn.workspace_id, both as recorded,
and the email address as user.email fixed to **** when the session carries one
and omitted when it does not, so the mask says an address exists and is
withheld rather than that none was recorded. sarif (partialFingerprints),
ndjson (skarn.fingerprint), evidence, and the json cross\-session chains
(fingerprint and exfilFingerprint) carry the finding fingerprint: the sha256
of the rule id, a NUL byte, and the lowercase hex sha256 of the
ASCII\-lowercased secret. That hash is deterministic and unkeyed, so a
guessable secret is recoverable from it by candidate testing wherever the
output travels. evidence is a redacted Markdown evidence pack for a product
technical file, and the one value that requires a Pro license. One of: text,
json, sarif, ndjson, evidence. The default is text.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Verbose output.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-color\fR
Disable colored output.
.TP
\fB\-\-policy\fR \fIpath\fR
Load a declarative TOML policy file: fail thresholds, required/forbidden
rules, baseline enforcement, and the output profile. The \-\-fail\-on\-* and
\-\-profile flags override it. A file skarn cannot read or parse exits 4
rather than falling back to the embedded default, which gates nothing.
Requires a Pro license.
.TP
\fB\-\-profile\fR \fIpreset\fR
Output profile. enterprise keeps high\-confidence findings and dedups them.
One of: enterprise. Requires an Enterprise license.
.TP
\fB\-\-fail\-on\-severity\fR \fIlevel\fR
Exit 1 if any finding is at or above this severity. One of: low, medium, high,
critical.
.TP
\fB\-\-fail\-on\-risk\fR \fI0\-100\fR
Exit 2 if the session risk score exceeds this threshold.
.TP
\fB\-\-fail\-on\-scan\-error\fR
Fail closed with exit 6 when the scan was incomplete: a tool source failed
discovery, a session store that exists could not be read, a session failed to
parse, a session had truncated or compacted transcript content so it no longer
holds the whole conversation (the assistant compacted it before the scan, or a
compliance transcript export carried a truncated tool block, a truncated text
block, or a compaction marker), a compliance transcript export withheld a turn
as unavailable or carried a client\-asserted turn the feed did not verify, or
the scan read no sessions. Truncated or compacted content counts sessions,
because a cut block is a partial turn and nobody can count what a compaction
removed; withheld and unverified content counts turns, because the export
names each one. Every truncated tool block counts its session, including a
block returned as a placeholder instead of cut text, so a coverage gap is
over\-reported rather than missed. By default a partial scan still reports,
and an empty scan still warns on stderr.
.TP
\fB\-\-baseline\fR \fIpath\fR
Suppress findings recorded in this baseline, so the scan fails only on new
ones. A single file is free; a directory unions every .json baseline inside
it, an org\-shared accepted set, and requires a Pro license. That union leaves
out any fingerprint a member labelled a true positive. Without this flag skarn
applies ~/.config/skarn/baseline.json when it exists, for suppression only;
the \-\-baseline\-create and \-\-baseline\-merge targets stay explicit.
.TP
\fB\-\-baseline\-create\fR
Write the current findings to the \-\-baseline file as the accepted set, then
exit. Under \-\-fail\-on\-scan\-error an incomplete scan is refused instead:
the run exits 6 and leaves the existing file as it was.
.TP
\fB\-\-baseline\-merge\fR \fIfile\fR
Consolidate the \-\-baseline input (a file or a directory of per\-member
baselines) into one distributable file and exit. Requires a Pro license.
.TP
\fB\-\-audit\-log\fR \fIfile\fR
Append a hash\-chained record of this scan (timestamp, policy, finding counts,
verdict; no secrets) to this local log. The chain detects in\-place edits and
reordering of recorded history. Requires a Pro license.
.TP
\fB\-\-audit\-verify\fR \fIfile\fR
Verify an audit log's hash chain, then exit without scanning. A broken chain
exits 1. A log that exists but could not be read exits 6: a permission
failure, a directory, or a log past the read cap. An absent log is a valid
empty chain and exits 0. Needs no license.
.TP
\fB\-\-drop\fR \fIdir\fR
After the scan, write a self\-describing drop artifact to
<dir>/<identity>/<runId>.json: identity, org, host, run id, scan window, the
\-\-project scope it declares, and the redacted summary counts. The counts
cover the declared scope, not the machine, so the session count is the number
that passed \-\-project and \-\-session. Scope is recorded twice:
scope.projects is the \-\-project needles verbatim, often a path, and
scope.resolved is the project names those needles matched. Content is
attributed against scope.resolved, so a run that declared no scope resolves
nothing and therefore names no project at all. The assistant list and the
last\-activity timestamp describe the sessions the scan read inside that
scope, not the ones that produced a finding. The guard mode comes from the
same wired\-hook walk doctor performs, which only this flag pays for; an
absent guard mode means it could not be resolved, not that no hook is wired.
The write is atomic, so a second run in the same window adds a file rather
than overwriting one. The artifact carries a schema version and is never
reinterpreted across versions, so a release that moves that version needs the
machines upgraded alongside whatever assembles their artifacts, and the ones
written at the older version cleared out of the collection directory. Skarn
does not transmit it; moving the directory is the organization's job. Identity
comes from SKARN_IDENTITY, falling back to a hostname\-derived tag with a
warning.
.TP
\fB\-\-product\fR \fIname\fR
Release lineage for \-\-format evidence: the product this scan's sessions
changed. Without lineage, the evidence pack states that it cannot be attached
to a specific technical file.
.TP
\fB\-\-product\-version\fR \fIver\fR
Release lineage for \-\-format evidence: the product version the pack
describes.
.TP
\fB\-\-build\-id\fR \fIid\fR
Release lineage for \-\-format evidence: the commit or build identifier the
pack describes.
.TP
\fB\-\-sbom\-ref\fR \fIref\fR
Release lineage for \-\-format evidence: a reference to the product's declared
SBOM, recorded as supplied (control characters masked). Skarn neither parses
nor fetches it.
.TP
\fB\-\-sbom\fR \fIpath\fR
Reconcile production dependencies the sessions added to a manifest against
this declared SBOM, in CycloneDX or SPDX 2.x JSON format. Each undeclared
addition is reported as low\-severity workflow\-drift evidence, so pair it
with \-\-severity low. Unlike \-\-sbom\-ref, skarn reads and parses the file;
an unreadable or malformed one fails the scan.
.TP
\fB\-\-user\fR \fIname\fR
Restrict the scan to matching users: a comma\-separated list of user ids or
email addresses, where an id matches exactly and an email address matches
case\-insensitively. Only a session read from a Compliance API export carries
either, so on a machine without an export, or under a registered license below
Enterprise, every \-\-user value matches nothing and the report is empty at
exit 0. The drop artifact and the evidence pack do not record a user
narrowing, so a narrowed run reads like a whole\-corpus one there.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
\fB0\fR
Clean, or informational output only. For mcp: the client closed stdin, which
is the normal shutdown.
.TP
\fB1\fR
A finding at or above the \-\-fail\-on\-severity threshold was reported. For
setup and guard \-\-self\-test: a target was refused or a verification failed.
For doctor: at least one check FAILED (a warning never exits nonzero), or an
unknown option was passed, which is refused before any check runs. For
restore: the recorded working directory or session id could not be rendered
safely, or the working directory carries a detected secret, so the resume
command cannot be printed, nothing was printed to stdout and the refusal is
named on stderr. Also: the first\-run EULA prompt was declined, so the invoked
operation was not performed. For mcp: an unknown argument was passed, which is
refused before the protocol loop starts. For license: an install or a renewal
was refused or did not complete, for example because an argument was invalid,
a license did not verify, a replacement was declined, there was no license to
renew, the renewal endpoint was refused or could not be reached, or a file
could not be read or written. A failure before the new license is written
leaves the installed license as it was.
.TP
\fB2\fR
The session risk score exceeded the \-\-fail\-on\-risk threshold. For setup:
no terminal to prompt on and no \-\-yes; the exact non\-interactive command is
printed. For license: the license service declined the renewal that license
renew asked for, and nothing was written.
.TP
\fB3\fR
A canary token was triggered (a proven breach). This overrides every other
gate. For license: license renew refused the license the service returned,
because it did not verify, could not be used (revoked or expired, for
example), or did not supersede the license being renewed or the one already at
the destination, so nothing was written.
.TP
\fB4\fR
A policy precondition was not met: a required rule is missing, the policy
requires a baseline and none was supplied or the one supplied did not load, or
the \-\-policy file itself could not be read or parsed. A policy that never
loaded is never quietly replaced by the embedded default, which gates nothing.
.TP
\fB5\fR
A requested paid feature is not covered by the active license (a Pro or
Enterprise flag without a license that covers it). Refused before any scan.
With no usable license at all, check exits 7 before this gate is consulted.
.TP
\fB6\fR
The scan could not complete: session discovery or the scan itself failed,
including a home directory that could not be resolved so no session store was
ever located, or the detection rules could not be loaded so no scan was ever
defined. Under \-\-fail\-on\-scan\-error an otherwise\-complete run exits 6 as
well whenever coverage was partial: a tool source dropped out of discovery, a
session store that exists could not be read, a session's parse failed, the
assistant compacted a session before the scan so its transcript no longer
holds the whole conversation, or the run read no sessions at all. That verdict
is computed BEFORE the report is rendered, so the SARIF invocation exit code,
the evidence pack and the audit record all carry 6 rather than describing the
run as clean and then exiting nonzero. A \-\-baseline\-create run is refused
on the same condition before it writes anything, so an incomplete scan is
never recorded as the accepted set and the previous baseline survives. For
check \-\-audit\-verify: the audit log exists but could not be read, so the
hash chain was never examined (an absent log is a valid empty chain and exits
0). For vet: the home directory could not be resolved (so the configuration
was never located at all), or a config file that exists was not read \- it
could not be read, or it was skipped because it is not that assistant's live
configuration \- so the configuration was only partly seen. For doctor: the
home directory could not be resolved, so every per\-assistant check would have
read an empty machine \- a diagnostic that could not be produced is never
reported as a healthy one. Fail\-closed \- the result is not trustworthy, so
it is never reported as clean.
.TP
\fB7\fR
No usable license is present \- none was found via SKARN_LICENSE,
SKARN_LICENSE_FILE, or the installed file \- so check, baseline audit, fleet
aggregate or fleet readout refused before scanning or reading a file. The free
license is issued at https://getskarn.com/free after a quick email
confirmation and is verified offline. Also used when a license IS present but
cannot be used at all (revoked, tampered, or signed by an untrusted key).
assess, baseline accept, guard accept, the recall commands, guard, setup,
doctor, vet, mcp, license, taxonomies and check \-\-audit\-verify never exit
7.
.SH SECURITY
Skarn scans locally and makes no network connection by default. Secrets are
redacted in check's reports, in everything the recall commands print (search,
recent, messages, stats, cmds, tools, mcps, restore, export), in the ndjson
stream, the evidence pack, the artifacts written by \-\-drop, serve and
assess, the baseline files, the mcp tools, the guard, and every serve API
route that returns session content. No flag lifts the mask.
.PP
Two commands transmit anything off the machine, and only when you type them.
skarn check \-\-update\-rules fetches a signed feed bundle from the subscriber
channel, presenting $SKARN_FEED_TOKEN as a bearer credential; \-\-feed\-url
names that channel when $SKARN_FEED_URL does not. The feed is off by default
and is disabled by \-\-offline. skarn license renew posts the installed
license token to the license service to receive a re\-signed one. Both refuse
any endpoint that is not https, or http against loopback, and any endpoint
carrying userinfo. No session content, no finding, and no scanned secret ever
leaves the machine through a connection skarn makes. skarn mcp makes no
network connection; the results it hands to the connected MCP client (redacted
findings by default, redacted session content only with \-\-enable\-recall)
are forwarded by that client to its model provider \- that egress is the
client's, and it happens only when you configure the server.
.PP
skarn vet reads the AI assistant configuration files listed under FILES
read\-only: it opens each one for reading, never writes, rewrites, or removes
one, and makes no network call. It reports what it finds and leaves every
decision about the configuration to you. \-\-offline is accepted for parity
and changes nothing.
.PP
skarn mcp reads the same session stores as assess and the same configuration
files as vet, read\-only and with no network call, and answers only the AI
coding assistant that spawned it over stdin and stdout. Its results are the
redacted scan report, the masked vet report, and session metadata carrying
neither message text nor any label authored inside a transcript; with
\-\-enable\-recall the two content tools add session text and labels with
every detected secret masked, and the search response carries no echo of the
query or of the project filter. Because the assistant receives those results,
they travel wherever that assistant sends its context, so treat them as
leaving the machine even though skarn itself opens no socket.
